{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789376039,"nonce":"H39oiaicc2n7pU2Ed/jZP3h7JcE=","message":"\"Close all these gaps\": the round after construct.\n\nTyped callbacks. Ash 41b4d18 (issue 6622407 there) gives a host entry by record a closure form: `RecordClosure` and `hlp_alloc_record_closure`, with `fun_record` one naked entry for every signature that places the argument registers by the closure's pattern and calls the entry; the interpreter recognises it as it does `fun_var_args`, and a dynamic call goes through the closure's full type. Here, an export may name a function's shape, `adder() -\u003e Fn(Num) -\u003e Num` (`TypeRef::Function`, the export grammar with balanced parentheses, the macro emitting `Float -\u003e Float` and returning a function in a register), and `callback::function_for_typed` makes a record closure over a `Callback` object holding the function and the kinds, of the program's own type so a call site compares pointers and calls directly. The var-args closure remains for `Fn` and for shapes the registers cannot take. Haxe→Wren closure: 54 → 37; what is left is wren_lift's `call_found_closure` (a quarter), argument crossing and the bridge's call.\n\nWren→Haxe closure 49 → 42: `direct_call` keyed its kinds by the closure's type through a lock-free list (`kinds_for` was an `RwLock`; before that `kinds_of` recomputed per call), and `hlp_setup_trap_in` takes the caller's lock depth instead of asking the seam (the bridge never calls in holding the GC lock; a debug assertion says so).\n\nConstruct. wren_lift b4e46fd (issue 363b393 there): the compiled-constructor path fetches the JIT state once, like a send, instead of seven times, and fills only the arguments it has. The core: drop-hooked objects (refs) bump through a region of their own, so the sweep's drop pass walks only their blocks (`Tlab::hooked_*`, `tlab_blocks` keyed by thread and kind); `trace_object` clears `PENDING` on what the core reaches, so the between-phases pass reads no side table; a dead ref skips the CAS on an object its cycle already forgot; a direct send and every protocol entry find the VM through the object's record (`vm_of`), not `VM_HERE`. Haxe→Wren construct 105 → 92, Wren→Haxe 79 → 76, Wren→Wren 27 → 28.\n\nEach cell alone, ns, at a load of ~7: static 26 / 38, method 22 / 40, getter 14 / 22, setter 17 / 35, closure 37 / 42, construct 92 / 76 (Haxe→Wren / Wren→Haxe); Wren→Wren construct 28.\n\nWhat the remaining costs are, in order: on every Wren→Haxe cell the trap (~8, `_setjmp` and the exception-state thread-local), Ash's typed call by kinds (~8), the host entry's crossing (~7), the send (~6); on every Haxe→Wren cell wren_lift's `call_found_closure` (~9, context swap and module-variable lookup per call), the slot's entry and the bridge's call (~8), the receiver check and argument crossing (~5); on construct the allocations and their zeroing (~15), the Wren cycle's per-object work for a ref'd object (~12: the pending traversal through wren_lift's visitor, and the drop pass), and wren_lift's constructor entry (~10). Thread-locals that remain are the runtimes' own: the core's TLAB per allocation, wren_lift's `ACTIVE_HEAP` per allocation and its JIT state per send, Ash's exception state per trap.","files":null}]}